      "title": "How Volcanoes Build Islands",
      "text": "How Volcanoes Build Islands\n\nMost oceanic islands begin far below the surface of the sea. Magma rising through fractures in the ocean floor erupts as lava that cools rapidly in cold seawater, forming rounded masses known as pillow basalts. Each eruption adds a new layer to the pile, and over hundreds of thousands of years the accumulated rock may grow into a seamount thousands of meters tall. Only when the summit finally breaches the surface does the structure become an island, though by then the great majority of its bulk remains submerged.\n\nThe source of the magma differs from place to place. Along volcanic arcs, one tectonic plate descends beneath another, and water released from the sinking slab lowers the melting point of the overlying mantle. The resulting magma is rich in silica and often erupts explosively, producing steep, ash-covered cones. Elsewhere, islands form over hot spots, columns of unusually warm mantle rock that appear to remain nearly fixed while the plate above them moves. Hot spot lavas are typically fluid and low in silica, and they spread outward to build broad shields with gentle slopes.\n\nThe motion of the plate over a stationary hot spot leaves a distinctive signature. As a volcano is carried away from its magma source, its eruptions cease, and a new center becomes active behind it. The result is a chain of islands whose ages increase steadily with distance from the currently active volcano. Geologists use such chains to estimate the speed and direction of plate movement over millions of years, since the spacing of the extinct cones records how far the plate traveled between eruptions.\n\nConstruction is only half the story. Once eruptions stop, waves, rain, and landslides begin to strip the island away, while the cooling oceanic crust beneath it slowly subsides. In tropical waters, reef-building corals may grow upward as fast as the island sinks. When the volcanic peak finally disappears, a ring of reef and low sandy islets can remain, marking the outline of a mountain that is no longer there.",
