        "text": "Why Leaves Change Color\n\nEach autumn in the temperate forests of the Northern Hemisphere, the foliage of deciduous trees shifts from uniform green to a range of yellows, oranges, and reds. The transformation is not a sudden creation of new material so much as the unmasking and, in some cases, the manufacture of pigments within the leaf. Understanding the process requires attention to three classes of compounds: chlorophyll, carotenoids, and anthocyanins.\n\nChlorophyll, the pigment responsible for capturing light energy during photosynthesis, absorbs red and blue wavelengths and reflects green, which is why active leaves appear green. Chlorophyll molecules are chemically unstable and degrade continuously in sunlight, so a tree must synthesize them constantly through the growing season to maintain a functioning canopy. This synthesis depends on warmth and on a steady supply of water and minerals. As days shorten in late summer, a layer of cells called the abscission layer begins to form at the base of each leaf stalk, gradually restricting the movement of materials between leaf and branch. Chlorophyll production slows and then halts, and the existing supply breaks down.\n\nCarotenoids, which reflect yellow and orange light, are present in the leaf throughout the growing season but are ordinarily concealed by the far more abundant chlorophyll. When the green pigment disappears, these compounds are simply revealed. Anthocyanins behave differently. In most species they are not present in summer leaves at all; they are produced in autumn from sugars trapped in the leaf by the developing abscission layer. Their formation therefore depends on conditions that favor sugar accumulation, notably bright days coupled with cool, though not freezing, nights.\n\nThis dependence explains why red displays vary considerably from year to year while yellow displays are comparatively reliable. A warm, overcast autumn tends to yield muted reds, since less sugar is retained and converted. Prolonged drought or an early hard frost can shorten the season altogether by hastening leaf fall before the pigments are fully expressed.",
